; AX3042: silence is a CLAIM, and this is it being refuted.
;
; Effects were opt-in until 2026-08-25: a function that volunteered no
; AXTAG was never asked what it performed, so a body could reach the
; outside world and nothing in the build would say so. An untagged
; function now claims to perform no IO, and that claim is checked like
; any other.
;
; Every arm of the rule is here, because the ones that must stay
; SILENT are what keep it from being a blanket refusal:
;
;   `leaks`    performs IO, says nothing            -> AX3042
;   `declares` performs IO and says so              -> silent
;   `builds`   allocates and mutates, says nothing  -> silent
;   `lies`     performs IO under `pure`             -> AX3010, not AX3042
;
; `builds` is the measured half. Of 3,040 functions in `self_host/`
; and `stdlib/`, 1,911 perform something and 1,382 perform exactly
; `Alloc,Mut` - every function that touches a String or a Vec, because
; allocating is what an expression DOES here and `Mut` is set by
; writing any struct field. Requiring a declaration on those is 1,382
; annotations that distinguish nothing from nothing, so `Alloc` and
; `Mut` are ambient: still inferred, still on the AXSYM line, still
; measured by `handle`/`(Pure)`, and never declarable.
;
; `lies` pins the split between the two codes. A declaration that made
; a claim belongs to AX3010 whether the claim is right or wrong;
; AX3042 is only for one that made no claim at all, so no declaration
; ever draws both.
(import IO)

(import Vec)

(:: leaks (-> Int Int))

(fn (leaks n)
  {
    (println "out")
    n
  }
)

(:: declares (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (declares n)
  {
    (println "out")
    n
  }
)

(:: builds (-> Int Int))

(fn (builds n)
  (let ((v vecNew))
    {
      (vecPush v n)
      (vecLen v)
    }
  )
)

(:: lies (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:pure
(fn (lies n)
  {
    (println "out")
    n
  }
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (declares 1))
